{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790467655,"nonce":"Q1HvDPTspfyJ4u5FOzuvYnWM8/Q=","title":"Benchmarks Game kernels: where a published Wren JIT is ahead","message":"8bcb8b2 added seven Benchmarks Game kernels at the sizes lulzx.com/WrenJIT\npublishes. Tiered wlift on arm64 (local, one run) against that page's\nWrenJIT numbers (its machine, 2026-08-08), in ms:\n\n| kernel | wlift | WrenJIT | stock Wren |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| spectral_norm | 72 | 17 | 869 |\n| pidigits | 325 | 66 | 1399 |\n| reverse_complement | 24 | 12 | 88 |\n| fannkuch_redux | 1819 | 1790 | 7990 |\n| fasta | 28 | 55 | 116 |\n| k_nucleotide | 164 | 175 | 249 |\n| regex_redux | 391 | 456 | 877 |\n\nNot the same machine, so only the large ratios mean anything: spectral_norm\n(4x) and pidigits (5x). WrenJIT's page says its loop compiler removes\ndispatch, boxing, redundant guards and loop-carried memory traffic, and\nreduces some loops as recurrences. Worth profiling ours on those two:\nspectral_norm's inner loop is a static call per element (denominator) and a\nfloat divide; pidigits is list reads/writes with % and floor per element.","files":null}]}